# ThreadSanitizer suppressions (M22, D-072). Each entry names code TSan cannot
# see the synchronisation of, with the reason. Keep this list short.
#
# rte_hash's lock-free lookup pairs its key compare with a version counter
# read in DPDK code built without TSan instrumentation; TSan sees only the
# BESS-side compare of the key bytes (decisions.md, M9 TSan run).
race:CmpFixed
# rte_ring (DPDK 25.11): element copies are ordered by the head/tail protocol
# through relaxed loads and standalone fences (a consumer after the producer,
# a producer after the consumer that freed the slot), which ThreadSanitizer
# does not model; it reports the copies themselves. The handoff channel's own
# counters, ownership and packets stay instrumented.
race:__rte_ring_dequeue_elems
race:__rte_ring_enqueue_elems
# rte_lpm: a tbl8 group is reused only after a grace period, ordered by the
# QSBR defer queue inside librte_lpm (uninstrumented); TSan sees the group's
# re-initialising memset and an inline lookup's read, not the grace period
# between them. The route tests check every concurrent answer (D-046, D-047).
# Suppressed by the writer (the tbl8 group's re-initialisation in librte_lpm),
# not the inline reader: a race of any other writer against a FIB lookup --
# a BESS table freed or reset under readers -- is still reported.
race:add_depth_big
# The transaction engine's lock and the Router's are taken in both orders, and
# both nestings are safe because a single lock above them serialises every path
# that takes either pair: the control plane's mutex, held by
# ApplyTransaction/ApplyPipeline on the transaction side and by
# CreateModule/DestroyModule on the Init/DeInit side, and only one thread holds
# it. The cycle TSan reports (D-158 introduced the second edge):
#   Router::Enroll (router.cc:350) -> ResourceRegistry::Register ->
#     TransactionEngine::Register (transaction_engine.cc:65)  [router -> engine]
#   TransactionEngine::ApplyImpl (transaction_engine.cc:483) ->
#     Router::RouteResource::Reserve (router.cc:190)          [engine -> router]
# Its deadlock detector works on the observed lock-order graph and cannot see
# that outer lock. Suppressed at both ends of the newer edge: a cycle that does
# not pass through the router's mutex and the engine's is still reported.
deadlock:bess::route::Router::RouteResource::Reserve
deadlock:bess::route::Router::Enroll
